---
import ServiceSection from "../../components/ServiceSection.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ServiceLayout from "../../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro";
---

<Layout>
	<ServiceLayout
		eyebrow="Custom Software"
		title="Custom Software Development"
		intro="Purpose-built systems for the way your company actually works."
		highlights={[
			"Discovery & technical strategy",
			"Product design & architecture",
			"Web development",
			"Systems integration",
			"Ongoing optimization",
		]}
	>
		<ServiceSection
			heading="From operational needs to a clear product direction"
		>
			<p>
				Nightmare Software helps companies turn real operational challenges into
				software that supports the way their teams actually work. The process
				starts with discovery: understanding current workflows, systems,
				constraints, user roles, and the decisions the software needs to
				support. This may include reviewing existing tools, mapping handoffs,
				identifying repetitive work, and separating essential requirements from
				assumptions. The result is a practical foundation for deciding what to
				build and why.
			</p>
		</ServiceSection>
		<ServiceSection heading="Architecture and product design">
			<p>
				With the problem space defined, Nightmare Software shapes an
				architecture that can support the product's intended use, integrations,
				data flows, security needs, and future change. Product and interface
				design are grounded in the workflows uncovered during discovery, so
				screens, permissions, and system behavior reflect how users operate
				rather than forcing them into a generic process. Technical decisions are
				made with attention to maintainability, reliability, and the realities
				of the company's existing environment.
			</p>
		</ServiceSection>
		<ServiceSection heading="Building software that can evolve">
			<p>
				Development turns the agreed direction into working software through an
				iterative process. Nightmare Software can help establish the application
				structure, implement core capabilities, connect required services, and
				create the supporting data and deployment foundations. Work is organized
				so that assumptions can be reviewed as the product takes shape, allowing
				feedback from stakeholders and users to inform subsequent decisions
				instead of waiting until the end of a long, inflexible build.
			</p>
		</ServiceSection>
	</ServiceLayout>
</Layout>
